  1391  /*!
  1392   * \brief   pixConvertRGBToColormap()
  1393   *
  1394   * \param[in]    pixs       32 bpp rgb
  1395   * \param[in]    ditherflag  1 to dither, 0 otherwise
  1396   * \return  pixd 2, 4 or 8 bpp with colormap, or NULL on error
  1397   *
  1398   * <pre>
  1399   * Notes:
  1400   *      (1) This function has two relatively simple modes of color
  1401   *          quantization:
  1402   *            (a) If the image is made orthographically and has not more
  1403   *                than 256 'colors' at the level 4 octcube leaves,
  1404   *                it is quantized nearly exactly.  The ditherflag
  1405   *                is ignored.
  1406   *            (b) Most natural images have more than 256 different colors;
  1407   *                in that case we use adaptive octree quantization,
  1408   *                with dithering if requested.
  1409   *      (2) If there are not more than 256 occupied level 4 octcubes,
  1410   *          the color in the colormap that represents all pixels in
  1411   *          one of those octcubes is given by the first pixel that
  1412   *          falls into that octcube.
  1413   *      (3) If there are more than 256 colors, we use adaptive octree
  1414   *          color quantization.
  1415   *      (4) Dithering gives better visual results on images where
  1416   *          there is a color wash (a slow variation of color), but it
  1417   *          is about twice as slow and results in significantly larger
  1418   *          files when losslessly compressed (e.g., into png).
  1419   * </pre>
  1420   */
  1421  PIX *
  1422  pixConvertRGBToColormap(PIX     *pixs,
  1423                          l_int32  ditherflag)
